   "title": "Phone Box Libraries",
   "rubric": "You will hear part of a podcast about how old British phone boxes are used as libraries.\nFirst you will have one minute to study the task below, then you will hear the recording twice.\nWhile listening, choose the correct answer (A, B, C or D) for questions 1-7.\nOnly one answer is correct.\nThe first one (0) has been done for you.\nAfter the second listening, you will have 1 minute to check your answers.",
   "audio": "audio/inv10-l1.mp3",
   "mins": 10,
   "transcript": "Once Superman’s changing room, the humble phone box has seen many uses beyond actual telephony… and now all over the UK, people are turning iconic British red telephone boxes into micro libraries. These otherwise abandoned kiosks are now places where anyone can feel free to choose a book and take it home. The only rule is that you bring it back when you have finished reading it or you can keep it as long as you replace it with another book. There is no registration and there are no fines for late returns. The idea came into existence in response to the continuing threat facing public libraries in the UK. The first such telephone box library was set up in Westbury-Sub-Mendip in Somerset in 2009 after the local council cut funding for the area’s mobile library. The local council purchased the box, a Giles Gilbert Scott K6 design, for £1, and residents in the Somerset village installed wooden shelves inside and donated their own books. The phone box’s collection now includes titles that range from cookery to children’s books and from blockbusters to the classics.\nA similar episode occurred in South London, where a local man called Seb Handley purchased an unused telephone box that was in a very poor condition from British Telecom for £1. Using his own money and repairing skills to renovate the box, he turned it into one of London’s smallest libraries. The library became an excuse for people to stand around chatting as well as a source of literature.\nThese micro-library exchanges operate on a system of trust. In local villages across England, where everybody knows everybody else, this seems to have been a relatively stress free and functional initiative. However, in some larger cities, the microlibraries have on occasion had to rely on the local community to step in when the libraries have been vandalised.\nWhile British Telecom have said they will not be selling any more of their famous red telephone boxes, people who want to do something similar and set up their own mini libraries can create their own little free libraries.\nWe are reminded of the beliefs behind these libraries by the worldwide ‘Little Free Libraries’ that were founded by Todd Boll in Wisconsin, America. Boll made a small wooden house, just large enough for twenty books and put it at the end of his drive with a sign saying ‘Free Books’ on it. It didn’t take long for the idea to spread across America and now there are ‘Little libraries’ all over the world. It’s possible to find helpful instructions on how to create your own library box online. So, come on, all of you book worms. Give all your old books a new life. Happy reading, friends!",
