   "title": "Mindfulness",
   "rubric": "Listen to Martin Rogers, a health correspondent, talking about mindfulness.\nFirst you will have one minute to study the task below, then you will hear the recording twice.\nWhile listening, match the beginnings of the sentences (1-7) with the sentence endings (A-J).\nThere are two sentence endings that you should not use.\nThe first one (0) has been done for you.\nAfter the second listening, you will have one minute to check your answers.",
   "audio": "audio/inv10-l2.mp3",
   "mins": 10,
   "transcript": "Have you ever travelled somewhere and then noticed that you couldn’t remember anything about the journey? Or opened a packet of biscuits intending to eat one or two but end up eating the whole packet? When we do these things, we are not fully awake and therefore, not totally living our lives. These are examples of mindlessness, but what exactly is mindfulness?\nWell, when we are mindful, we are more awake. We know what our thoughts and actions are, and we know what is happening around us. We might notice a beautiful landscape or listen carefully to what a friend is saying, rather than thinking about what we’re going to say next. We also have a better understanding of our own feelings and thoughts. Jon Kabat Zinn, who is working hard to make mindfulness popular, says mindfulness is: ‘Paying attention, on purpose, in the present moment.’\nThis means we are intentionally deciding what to pay attention to, we are not worrying about the past or planning for the future and we are not trying to control or stop our thoughts or feelings – we’re just noticing them.\nThanks to technology, we always have something to do and there isn’t much opportunity to just ‘be’. We regularly do two or three things at the same time like texting while watching TV, or looking at our phone while walking along the road. All this can make us stressed. Mindfulness can help to reduce this stress.\nLet’s look at the benefits of mindfulness.\nIt can help us to increase our attention levels, improve our memory, reduce stress and depression and to think more clearly. Relationships can be more satisfying and we can be more creative. It can help people to handle pain better and to get more sleep. It can even help you lose weight because you will think twice before eating a whole packet of biscuits! All of these will undoubtedly lead to a happier life.\nSo, what can we do to become more mindful?\nA very simple technique that you could try right now is to close your eyes for a couple of minutes and count how many sounds you can hear. This will help you to focus on what is happening right now. Another technique is to focus on a piece of food, a dried grape, for example. Instead of eating it without thinking, look at it carefully and notice how it feels in your fingers. Smell it and then put it on your tongue and taste it. Start to eat it slowly and notice how it feels and tastes. Both of these techniques force you to slow down and focus on the present moment.",
