   "title": "Clothing Resale - The Answer to Sustainability?",
   "rubric": "Listen to a radio broadcast about clothing resale.\nFirst you will have one minute to study the task below, then you will hear the recording twice.\nWhile listening, answer the questions (1-9) using a maximum of four words.\nWrite your answers in the spaces provided.\nThe first one (0) has been done for you.\nAfter the second listening, you will have one minute to check your answers.",
   "audio": "audio/inv10-l4.mp3",
   "mins": 10,
   "transcript": "Second-hand clothing is by no means a recent phenomenon.\nCharity or Thrift shops have been around for decades. Years ago, buying second-hand goods was considered embarrassing, unpleasant and even unhealthy. When clothes began to be mass produced and, therefore, cheaper people saw clothing as a disposable commodity, leading to tons of waste. This is when some stores started to reuse these materials to help the poor and homeless rather than throw them away. While the creation of thrift stores was originally for lower-income buyers, people started going to them in search of vintage high fashion items, looking for something beautiful from the past. The steadily rising popularity and appeal of thrifting has brought shoppers of various ages and social-economic backgrounds to thrift stores. Nowadays the process of buying second-hand clothes from thrift stores is even easier thanks to the internet. Many shoppers had already stopped being interested in traditional clothing stores for both sustainability and cost reasons before the Covid-19 pandemic, but global lockdowns greatly boosted online sales. Post-pandemic traditional shopping was challenging as many people had no desire to search through the racks again. In 2019, one quarter of global shoppers bought an item of used clothing and the industry’s share of the overall clothing market is growing fast.\nMajor investors have been quick to realise the growth potential of the online second-hand and vintage market putting money into sites such as Vestaire Collective and Poshmark.\nThe second-hand market is expected to double in a very short time. But who is buying all these second-hand clothes?\nWell, younger consumers are powering the trend. In fact, they are not only buying used clothes online but they are also selling their own unwanted clothes on sites like ebay and Vinted. The online resale market is naturally getting crowded. Larger companies are pushing their way into the market and are fighting each other for a place at the top. However, it hasn’t all been plain sailing. Traditional high street stores are fighting back. They are trying to appeal to those of us who still enjoy the thrill of discovering that special item on the shelves, something that cannot be experienced when browsing online. Fast fashion stores and websites such as China’s Shein and the UK’s Primark are still doing very well. Sweden’s H&M not only sells cheap new clothing but also collects old garments for recycling and several of its brands have entered the resale market. Despite the challenges, the online resale market is getting stronger rather than fading away. Another reason for the success of second-hand clothing businesses is that the fashion industry is under great pressure to reduce its greenhouse gas emissions. The industry accounts for about 10 per cent of worldwide emissions due to long supply chains and the use of petroleum-based synthetics. This makes second-hand sales more attractive to customers. After all, the best way to reduce total carbon emissions is to resell the same goods. Although clothing resale will not solve the sustainability problem, it will definitely help it.",
