   "rubric": "Listen to an English language teacher asking some students about the effect emojis and GIFs are having on how we use our language and the way we communicate.\nFirst you will have one minute to study the task below.\nThen you will hear the recording twice.\nWhile listening, match the speakers (1-6) with the statements (A-H) that best represent their opinion.\nThere is one extra statement that you should not use.\nThe first one (0) has been done for you.\nAfter the second listening, you will have one minute to check your answers.",
   "audio": "audio/inv10-l5.mp3",
   "mins": 10,
   "transcript": "[0 – Teacher] Hi guys. Ok, so, today we’re going to talk about emojis and GIFs and how they are changing the way we use our language and the way we communicate. Even I always add an emoji at the end of messages to my friends. What do you think about it? Richard, let’s start with your opinion.\n[1 – Richard] I think emojis, GIFs and stickers are really useful for communicating ideas or emotions that we would otherwise find difficult to express. For example, when I’m chatting on social media they make the conversation funnier and more entertaining. I use them in emails, too, but only if I know the person I’m writing to well. If it was an important or formal email then I wouldn’t use them.\n[Teacher] Thanks. Judith, I see you want to say something…\n[2 – Judith] I think they are a good idea, too. Really, they are only a new version of phrases, like the hieroglyphics that were used by the Egyptians. Every language has changed with time and these latest additions help us to express our thoughts. If you think about it, pictures and symbols are how we understand the meaning of something and letters are the same. Emojis and GIFs are just another form of communication.\n[Teacher] Hum… interesting. Sarah?\n[3 – Sarah] In my opinion, emoji affect communication in some way but I believe they help make it more dynamic. I don’t think that they impede how we use our language at all. They help us to express our feelings quite well but, more importantly, quicker than I could if I were to write a whole sentence.\n[Teacher] Thanks Sarah. Charlie, your point of view?\n[4 – Charlie] I don’t agree with you. I think we should stop using emojis and GIFs and use words alone, abbreviated and regular, if not, we might forget how to use some of them in the future. I hate it when I write a message to someone and they reply with an emoji. I could misunderstand what they want to say because we all interpret pictures in a different way.\n[Teacher] Yes, I see your point. Ashley, your opinion?\n[5 – Ashley] I think that while they are great fun, they are restricting our language and communication a little. If we use them in our everyday lives then, instead of expanding our vocabulary, we are limiting it losing the richness of our language.\n[Teacher] Thank you. Fiona, I see you are puzzled…\n[6 – Fiona] Well… it’s because I agree with all of you… emojis and GIFs effect our language and communication in both good and bad ways. On the one hand, they reduce our use of the thousands of words in our language but on the other hand, if chosen carefully, they help us to communicate more specifically and they can be fun, too.",
