   "title": "Do Ants Really Produce Milk?",
   "rubric": "Listen to the documentary about the discovery of a milky substance produced by ants.\nFirst you will have one minute to study the task below, then you will hear the recording twice.\nWhile listening, match the beginnings of the sentences (1-8) with the sentence endings (A-K).\nThere are two sentence endings that you do not need.\nThe first one (0) has been done for you.\nAfter the second listening, you will have one minute to check your answers.",
   "audio": "audio/inv10-l6.mp3",
   "mins": 10,
   "transcript": "[Journalist] Ant colonies have been the subject of continuous research and fascination for over one hundred years.\nConsidered by some scientists to be a ‘superorganism’, the degree to which ants work together as a unit is fascinating.\nResearchers have mainly focused on the ants’ seemingly urgent and hurried activities. Rarely have they examined the pupal stage of the ant’s life cycle, which is when they transform from larvae - the immature stage - into adults.\nUntil now, that is. We are now joined by the myrmecologist Daniel Kronauer. Daniel, what exactly is a myrmecologists?\n[Daniel] A myrmecologist is a scientist who study ants.\n[Journalist] So, what new, exciting discovery has been made about ants?\n[Daniel] Ok, well, past research shows that the pupae don’t move, eat or do anything that is obvious in the middle of all the general excitement and activity going on in the colony.\nHowever, it has recently been recognised that the oftenunnoticed pupae provide a crucial service to the colony.\nTheir developing bodies produce a milk like substance that provides important nutrients to the other ants in the colony. And not just in one species but in at least one species in each of their five ant subfamilies. Such an extensive discovery means that the milky secretions could possibly be common in many more ant species and that this phenomenon may have originated early on in the evolution of all ants.\n[Journalist] That is absolutely fascinating. And who exactly discovered this?\n[Daniel] This captivating liquid was discovered by Susanne Foitzik, a postdoctoral fellow who had not worked with ants before doing her research. For this reason, her fresh eyes soon noticed behavioural characteristics that couldn’t be explained by the existing scientific literature. Rather than trying to understand the connected activity within the colony, where pupae and larvae are constantly being moved around and piled on top of each other, she decided to observe the pupae in isolation. It wasn’t easy working out how to keep them alive but eventually she found the right temperature and humidity level. Then she noticed that they were producing large amounts of a liquid. They were producing so much of it that many were dying because they were drowning in the liquid. But what she didn’t know was whether this was an abnormal episode because the pupae were isolated from the colony or something quite normal that had not been noticed previously.\n[Journalist] How did she find out why they were drowning?\n[Daniel] Well, to find out what exactly was happening, a blue food dye was injected into the pupae through the opening where the liquid was coming out. The pupae were then put back in the colony where adult ants were soon seen removing the liquid form the pupae and swallowing it. The blue colour was seen spreading through their digestive system. They also placed young larvae on the pupae which also fed on the liquid and turned blue, too. The liquid was subsequently analysed and found to contain all essential amino acids as well as multiple carbohydrates and some vitamins. The pupae of other insect species tend to reabsorb and recycle these nutritious liquids instead of sharing them and it is this difference that is thought to be the root of the intensely social lifestyle that ants have developed over millions of years.\n[Journalist] What does this new discovery tell us about the ant world?\n[Daniel] Ants depend on each other at different stages in their lives and this behaviour is like a kind of social glue that keeps them all together.",
